WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.700
How did anyone find a nuclear address, or prove what it does?

2
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.160
Chapter one showed receptors reading labels, with Ran controlling where cargo loads

3
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.160
and unloads.

4
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:19.260
Start with nucleoplasmin, a frog oocyte protein of five copies, each with a tail.

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.760
In 1982, Dingwall, Sharnick and Laskey clipped the tails away.

6
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.080
Bare cores injected into the cytoplasm stayed out.

7
00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.860
A larger pentamer keeping one tail went in.

8
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:37.213
One attached tail was sufficient for final accumulation.

9
00:00:38.593 --> 00:00:42.433
More tails changed the rate, not how much eventually reached the nucleus.

10
00:00:44.117 --> 00:00:47.517
One more injection put bare cores straight into the nucleus.

11
00:00:48.777 --> 00:00:49.897
They remained there.

12
00:00:51.177 --> 00:00:54.437
Why that injection matters is a question the page asks you.

13
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.140
Which route does cargo take?

14
00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:04.240
In 1984, Feldherr and colleagues injected gold grains coated with nucleoplasmin.

15
00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:08.320
The coated grains spanned about 5 to 20 nm.

16
00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:10.940
They entered the nucleus.

17
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.460
Electron microscopy located transport at the pores.

18
00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:16.460
And that's why we're here.

19
00:01:16.583 --> 00:01:23.003
Now predict. Coat the same gold with an inert polymer instead of nucleoplasmin.

20
00:01:24.323 --> 00:01:26.063
Where will most of the grains end up?

21
00:01:31.483 --> 00:01:33.243
Mostly outside the nucleus.

22
00:01:34.903 --> 00:01:40.083
Gold coated with nucleoplasmin lacking its transport part was also largely kept out.

23
00:01:41.283 --> 00:01:43.603
So the coat, not the gold, decided entry.

24
00:01:44.683 --> 00:01:46.683
Next, a label you could move.

25
00:01:48.243 --> 00:01:53.743
Kalderon, Smith and colleagues traced the address of SV40 large T antigen to a short

26
00:01:53.743 --> 00:02:00.423
stretch. The normally cytoplasmic reporters were beta β-galactosidase and pyruvate

27
00:02:00.423 --> 00:02:05.543
kinase. Attached to either one, the stretch caused nuclear accumulation.

28
00:02:06.833 --> 00:02:14.513
Next, change it. Swapping lysine 128 for threonine abolished nuclear accumulation.

29
00:02:15.733 --> 00:02:19.113
Moving, mutating, and blocking are complementary tests.

30
00:02:20.393 --> 00:02:22.573
Each result supports only a limited claim.

31
00:02:23.667 --> 00:02:28.507
By 1995, importin α was shown to recognize the signal.

32
00:02:29.727 --> 00:02:32.327
Importin β was shown to dock at the pore.

33
00:02:33.707 --> 00:02:36.567
Leucine-rich export signals still had no known reader.

34
00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:42.967
In 1997, Fornerod, Mattaj and colleagues tested CRM1.

35
00:02:44.247 --> 00:02:47.267
Extra CRM1 stimulated export of Rev.

36
00:02:48.407 --> 00:02:52.007
It also stimulated export of U snRNAs.

37
00:02:53.267 --> 00:02:56.007
Leptomycin B inhibited both export pathways.

38
00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:02.810
CRM1, RanGTP and an export signal formed a complex in vitro.

39
00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.770
All three results pointed at CRM1.

40
00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.550
Together, they made a stronger case than any one result alone.

41
00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:17.650
The same year, Izaurralde, Görlich and colleagues collapsed the Ran gradient in

42
00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:24.750
oocyte nuclei. They injected RanGAP1, RanBP1, or a defective Ran mutant.

43
00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.290
The mutant could not stably hold GTP.

44
00:03:29.483 --> 00:03:33.103
All three treatments depleted nuclear RanGTP.

45
00:03:34.443 --> 00:03:36.863
Major import and export pathways stopped.

46
00:03:38.563 --> 00:03:40.743
Export was more readily inhibited than import.

47
00:03:41.817 --> 00:03:44.057
None of these results proved everything.

48
00:03:45.517 --> 00:03:49.017
Move it, mutate it, block its reader: each supports one claim.

49
00:03:50.297 --> 00:03:52.477
The page's drug puzzle stays unresolved.
